Stage 4: README 5.2's floor arms.

commit:  d281acf6ae3d78510d62c68f969ff16fe52e0768 (+ this slice)
payloads: AK_BENCH_ONLY=P1.2,P1.3,P2.2,P2.5,P3.1,P6.1

  arm a   target implementation, target runtime .NET 8.0.31
          Enc.StringField transcodes with Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(ReadOnlySpan<char>, Span<byte>)
  arm b   FLOOR implementation, target runtime .NET 8.0.31
          Enc.StringField transcodes with the netstandard2.0 unsafe overload,
          Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(char*, int, byte*, int)
  arm c   floor implementation, floor runtime -- see stage4-floor-arm-c.log

One generator, one source tree, one define flipped (README 5.1). Both builds
pass the SAME 120 correctness checks, which is 5.1's binding condition: the
wire bytes are identical across target levels or the divergence is a defect.

a and b CANNOT share a process: they are two definitions of one method in one
assembly. R4's rule for that case is to say so and carry an in-process control
column, so both binaries carry gp-writeto and gp-parse, and the floor question
is asked as the difference between two managed/incumbent RATIOS rather than
between two absolutes. They are run round robin with a rotating order, which
is the shape the Rust slice's D18 forced on its own multi-build comparison.

